{"summary":"Accept all seven amendments. The first four address real consistency and modeling robustness issues in shared substrate/microfauna logic. The last three identify clear correctness defects in the new stage-based shrimp molt implementation: the mineral gate is too permissive under severe deficiency, mixed-stage outcomes corrupt shared failure state, and the migration seeding path misinitializes fresh stage timers.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-2-0142f03c","decision":"accept","rationale":"Both sites encode the same O2:C stoichiometric conversion for microfauna respiration. Duplicating that constant risks silent divergence between actual respiration routing and the substrate O2-demand estimate, which would undermine model consistency and be hard to detect."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-0571fa0c","decision":"accept","rationale":"The substrate estimator and the actual microfauna step both rely on the same 1% daily detritus-consumption assumption. Keeping that as duplicated magic numbers invites drift and would desynchronize estimated substrate demand from realized consumption behavior."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-6ea35aba","decision":"accept","rationale":"The per-habitat accessibility weights are duplicated in two paths that are meant to describe the same microfauna access pattern. If those tables drift, periphyton removal and substrate respiration estimates stop matching, so sharing the function is the safer and more maintainable design."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-54222a0f","decision":"accept","rationale":"`effective_substrate_porosity` currently represents only the first non-empty layer, not the effective stack. Once O2 penetration extends beyond the top layer, differing lower-layer porosity can materially skew diffusion estimates, so this needs either explicit documentation as an approximation or a layered calculation."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-9ceac44a","decision":"accept","rationale":"This is a real correctness bug. With GH, Ca, and Mg near zero, the 0.3 floors still produce a mineral factor of 0.3, and the default scoring formula lets otherwise healthy cohorts clear the 0.55 success threshold. That defeats the intended severe-deficiency molt gate."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-37123d6e","decision":"accept","rationale":"The new per-stage molt resolution writes back into one shared success/failure state, which is incorrect for mixed-stage days. A simultaneous success and failure can reduce `failed_molt_accum`, and a failure in one stage can wrongly block unrelated sub-adult maturation through the global `last_molt_success` flag."},{"amendment_id":"fr-2-7560ca22","decision":"accept","rationale":"The migration heuristic cannot distinguish legacy saves from fresh/default state because `AnimalState::default()` still sets `inter_molt_timer_days` to 14 while all stage timers default to zero. That means ordinary newly constructed stage cohorts get preloaded mid-cycle and can molt or fail earlier than intended."}]}